From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bootowser <b@o.local>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:13:09 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] [Bootowser] remove unused browser chrome

The kiosk shows a page. Everything else on the toolbox is chrome that
nobody in front of it can use, and that an operator cannot afford to have
one keystroke or one stray click away from the allowlist in nsDocShell:

  * the app menu (hamburger) and the Windows PanelUI button -- the one
    door to the whole browser UI, including the parts policy hides;
  * the menu bar, which F10 would otherwise un-hide;
  * the bookmarks toolbar, the star button, and the five accelerators
    that create, list or show bookmarks;
  * the sidebar and its launcher button -- which is where vertical tabs,
    the bookmarks library, the history list and the AI chat live;
  * the downloads panel button, the account/sync button, the zoom
    button, the toolbar overflow, the all-tabs list, and the crash
    reporter button.

Three layers, because they fail differently:

1. Elements are hidden, not deleted. The chrome JS looks these up by id
   and dereferences the result in several places, so removing the nodes
   turns a cosmetic change into a startup exception. display:none takes
   them out of the layout and the a11y tree, which is what "removed"
   means to anyone using the browser.

2. The accelerators are compiled out. Policy binds defaults, not keys:
   Ctrl+N, Ctrl+W, Ctrl+Q, Ctrl+H, Ctrl+B, Ctrl+Shift+B, Ctrl+D,
   Ctrl+S, Ctrl+P, Ctrl+Shift+A, Ctrl+Shift+Y, Ctrl+Shift+P,
   Ctrl+Shift+Delete, Ctrl+Shift+T/N, Ctrl+Shift+S, Ctrl+K, the AI and
   open-tabs sidebar keys and the history keys all still fired with the
   app menu gone. They are wrapped in #ifndef MOZ_BOOTOWSER, so an
   upstream build gets every one of them back.

3. F10 is handled in C++, in dom/xul/MenuBarListener.cpp, where the
   DisplayMenuBar policy cannot reach it. Gated the same way.

Three null-safety fixes fall out of removing key elements. Callers look
those keys up by id, and several dereference the result unconditionally:

  * browser-init.js removes key_privatebrowsing and key_quitApplication
    whenever private browsing is off or the quit shortcut is disabled --
    which is every kiosk start.
  * ShortcutUtils.prettifyShortcut() is handed those ids from the star
    button tooltip, the quit dialog and the bookmarks menu, and reads
    getAttribute() off the result.
  * eventMatchesKey() gets key_close from the send-to-device panel.

All three now treat a missing key as "no shortcut", which is what a
removed key means. Behaviour is unchanged for upstream builds, where the
elements are still there.

The new tab is a separate concern and needs no patch: NewTabPage=false
makes it about:blank, and browser.theme.content-theme=0 (policy, locked)
paints that blank document with the dark content theme.

Upstream builds are unaffected: every change is behind MOZ_BOOTOWSER or
behind a null check that upstream's own elements satisfy.
